        {
            "id": "verb-tenses",
            "title": "Verb Tenses",
            "icon": "⏱️",
            "intro": "Ilonggo expresses tense through prefixes added to the verb root — not through separate helper words like 'was' or 'will'. Master three prefixes and you can place any action in time.",
            "sheets": [
                {
                    "title": "The Core Prefix System",
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                        "Form",
                        "Prefix",
                        "Example (root: Kaon = eat)",
                        "Meaning"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                        [
                            "Imperative (command)",
                            "— (root alone)",
                            "Kaon!",
                            "Eat!"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Past",
                            "nag-",
                            "Nagkaon ako.",
                            "I ate."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Present / Ongoing",
                            "naga-",
                            "Nagakaon ako.",
                            "I am eating."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Future / Infinitive",
                            "mag-",
                            "Magkaon ako.",
                            "I will eat."
                        ]
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "title": "Prefix Contraction Rules",
                    "type": "note",
                    "content": "When mag- or nag- meets a root starting with certain consonants, the prefix contracts:\n• mag + naog → Manaog (not Maginaog)\n• nag + naog → Nagnaog (not Nagnagnaog)\nAlways apply the prefix to the ROOT, never to an already-prefixed form."
