        {
            "id": "markers",
            "title": "Markers (Ang / Sang / Sa)",
            "icon": "🏷️",
            "intro": "Markers are short words placed before nouns to show their role in the sentence. Think of them as a grammar GPS — they tell you whether a noun is the subject, object, or location.",
            "sheets": [
                {
                    "title": "The Three Core Markers",
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                        "Marker",
                        "Role",
                        "Example",
                        "Meaning"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                        [
                            "Ang",
                            "Subject marker ('the' for the focus)",
                            "Ang bata nagkaon.",
                            "The child ate."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Sang",
                            "Object marker ('the' for the thing acted on)",
                            "Nagkaon ako sang karne.",
                            "I ate meat."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Sa",
                            "Location / Direction marker ('at', 'in', 'to')",
                            "Nagkadto kami sa merkado.",
                            "We went to the market."
                        ]
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "title": "Quick Rule",
                    "type": "note",
                    "content": "• ANG = who or what the sentence is ABOUT (subject)\n• SANG = the thing being acted upon (object)\n• SA = where something is or where it's going (location)\n\nExample: Nagkaon ang bata sang karne sa kusina.\n(The child ate meat in the kitchen.)\n→ ang bata = subject, sang karne = object, sa kusina = location"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "pronouns",
            "title": "Pronouns",
            "icon": "👥",
            "intro": "Ilonggo has a crucial distinction in its 'we' pronoun that English does not: whether the listener is included or not.",
            "sheets": [
                {
                    "title": "Personal Pronouns",
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                        "Ilonggo",
                        "English",
                        "Notes"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                        [
                            "Ako",
                            "I / Me",
                            "First person singular"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Ikaw / Ka",
                            "You",
                            "'Ka' is the short enclitic form used after a verb"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Sia",
                            "He / She / It",
                            "Gender-neutral"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Kami",
                            "We (exclusive)",
                            "Does NOT include the person you're speaking to"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Kita",
                            "We (inclusive)",
                            "INCLUDES the person you're speaking to"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Kamo",
                            "You all / Y'all",
                            "Second person plural"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Sila",
                            "They / Them",
                            "Third person plural"
                        ]
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "title": "Demonstrative Pronouns",
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                        "Ilonggo",
                        "English",
                        "Distance"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                        [
                            "Ini",
                            "This",
                            "Near the speaker"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Ina",
                            "That",
                            "Near the listener"
                        ],
                        [
                            "Ato / Ina",
                            "That (over there)",
                            "Far from both"
                        ]
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "title": "Kami vs Kita — The Key Distinction",
                    "type": "note",
                    "content": "This is one of the most important distinctions in Ilonggo:\n\n• Kami magkadto. → 'We are going.' (but NOT you — you're being left behind)\n• Kita magkadto. → 'We are going.' (all of us, including you)\n\nGetting this wrong can accidentally sound rude!"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "negation",
            "title": "Negation (Indi / Wala)",
            "icon": "🚫",
            "intro": "Ilonggo has two words for 'no' or 'not', and which one you choose depends on the TIME of the action being negated.",
            "sheets": [
                {
                    "title": "Indi vs Wala",
                    "type": "table",
                    "headers": [
                        "Word",
                        "When to use",
                        "Example",
                        "Meaning"
                    ],
                    "rows": [
                        [
                            "Indi",
                            "Future actions, general inability, or refusal",
                            "Indi ako magkaon.",
                            "I will not eat."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Indi",
                            "Present inability or general 'no'",
                            "Indi ako makaon.",
                            "I cannot eat."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Wala",
                            "Past actions that did NOT happen",
                            "Wala ako nagkaon.",
                            "I did not eat."
                        ],
                        [
                            "Wala",
                            "Something that doesn't exist or isn't there",
                            "Wala sia.",
                            "He/she is not here."
                        ]
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "title": "Memory Trick",
                    "type": "note",
                    "content": "INDI = future / general = 'I won't' or 'I can't'\nWALA = past / existence = 'I didn't' or 'there isn't any'\n\nThink of WALA like 'zero' — it means there was nothing, it didn't exist, or it didn't happen."
                }
            ]
        },
